The failing test `testLang295` checks if the `contains` method in `StrBuilder` correctly determines if a character is present in the buffer. The current bug report mentions that there are instances in `StrBuilder` where `thisBuf.length` is used when `size` should be used instead.

Looking at the `contains` method in `StrBuilder`, we see that it iterates over `thisBuf` using `thisBuf.length`. If `thisBuf` is not resized properly when the buffer size changes, this could lead to the method looking beyond the actual content of the buffer.

To fix this issue, `thisBuf.length` should be replaced with `size` to ensure that the method only iterates over the actual content of the buffer.

Next, I will suggest the best locations to fix this issue.
The bug can be fixed by updating the `contains` method in `StrBuilder` as follows:

```java
public boolean contains(char ch) {
    char[] thisBuf = buffer;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        if (thisBuf[i] == ch) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

The best location to fix this issue is:
```
org.apache.commons.lang.text.StrBuilder.contains(char)
```

